{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive, methodological rule of 'critical rationalism' that selects the theory with the highest informational content and severest test survival, without assigning it higher probability of truth.","counterconditions":["If the agent assumes the future will resemble the past in the theory's success.","If the preference implies a higher degree of belief or subjective probability.","If the choice is justified by an expectation of future predictive success derived from past performance."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Methodological selection based on verisimilitude (truth-likeness) and informational content rather than probabilistic induction. The scientist acts on the theory that has withstood the most severe tests because it is the best current conjecture, not because it is likely to be true.","optional_refs":["fdac0b227abad496482e41e6152851ea225b4e74266a0d711f8164b17ef355f1","810b0b4bbe1af9839f14e09c0cf06a5ea903d56c55c889671bb26f21cde9bf47"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back into Popper's system because acting on a theory requires an implicit assumption that its past success is a reliable guide to its future utility, which is the definition of inductive reasoning.","counterconditions":["If the agent can act without any expectation of future consistency.","If 'corroboration' can be shown to have zero bearing on future decision-making utility.","If the choice is purely random or aesthetic rather than based on test history."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Pragmatic necessity forces an inductive bridge. To choose Theory A over Theory B for action (e.g., building a bridge) implies believing A is more likely to hold up under future stress, which translates corroboration into a proxy for probability.","optional_refs":["a6ea9327509e38ef25510894d8803071a36d8ab71715643407c4a567c59668b0","5c80190574995e7600639ac68613611d092e9f25a84106c4581ec08e37f4c059"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible only if reinterpreted as a 'decision under uncertainty' where corroboration serves as a tie-breaker among equally unproven conjectures, accepting the cost that this makes scientific progress a series of non-rational (but critical) guesses.","counterconditions":["If the convention itself is justified by its past success (circular/inductive).","If scientists feel compelled by logic rather than convention.","If the tie-breaker implies a difference in truth-value."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Decision theory applied to falsificationism. Since no theory is probable, choice is arbitrary unless constrained by the rule: 'Choose the most severely tested.' This rule is a convention, not a logical derivation, avoiding induction by admitting its own conventional status.","optional_refs":["d1d271871de8727a8c719b97c013eb59b199b2a89a26744cfa08afc905a7865c","8e311e181668cbda6a6d4584e3c474d67abda2e4d6724dcbcd5b07be698352c0"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's distinction collapses under the pressure of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness); if we prefer theory A over B, we must believe A is closer to the truth, which assigns a quasi-probabilistic weight to corroboration, effectively reinstating induction.","counterconditions":["If verisimilitude can be defined without reference to future success.","If 'closeness to truth' has no behavioral implications for preference.","If the preference is shown to be independent of truth-likeness claims."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Verisimilitude as a hidden probability metric. The concept of 'closer to truth' functions cognitively as a probability assignment, even if labeled otherwise. The preference for high corroboration is a heuristic for maximizing verisimilitude, which is an inductive inference.","optional_refs":["beabdaebcfb2e0a9329c87340d71cd6dc6b6de31a1a7f5c64a3b9fe85cb38311","22867cbbd4c7d03233b3678b5041582fab2cfe0519e1f44feeaeef1dacfe0bb9"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.78},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is a 'rational faith' in the growth of knowledge, defensible only as an ethical stance of the scientist rather than a logical consequence of evidence, costing Popper the claim that his system is purely logical.","counterconditions":["If the ethical stance can be derived logically from the definition of science.","If the preference is shown to be logically necessary rather than ethically chosen.","If the 'faith' is shown to be unnecessary for the practice of science."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Ethical imperatives of the scientific community. The choice to prefer corroborated theories is a moral commitment to the ideal of truth-seeking, not a logical deduction. It transforms the problem from epistemology to ethics.","optional_refs":["23eed044f5c987a0e96292fdc3168ea1ae3455994a6e5d26d6b204a808f28404","93a3a57e0a8c103c9ed0887d6c4fae98087841df61ab011e4e14ad144f12b5bb"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The problem dissolves if we view corroboration as a measure of 'informational content' retained after testing; we prefer the theory with more content because it says more about the world, regardless of its truth probability, making the preference non-inductive.","counterconditions":["If the preference for richness implies a belief that richer theories are more likely true.","If 'informational content' cannot be separated from 'probability of truth' in decision contexts.","If acting on a high-content theory requires assuming its truth."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Informational content maximization. Corroboration reports how much risky content has survived. We prefer the theory that risks more and survives, as it provides more information. This is a preference for richness, not likelihood.","optional_refs":["2e059135189855cf7e6237ae32e94548b68316951bcd55d5b5105128e14629b6","83a2cc5e9d1d76bda6053cda0c458179b82b7cc874281f3452b654564e26ded0"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}],"simulation_proposals":[]}